# Can a market work without money?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "How do kidney exchanges and school choice match people without prices? What did Roth's market design add to economics?"
next: {"title":"Should everything be for sale?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/econ-from-zero/62-should-everything-be-for-sale.cast"}

## A swap instead of a sale
sources:
    - id: rees2009
      title: A Nonsimultaneous, Extended, Altruistic-Donor Chain
      authors: Rees, M. A., Kopke, J. E., Pelletier, R. P., Segev, D. L., Rutter, M. E., Fabrega, A. J. et al.
      year: 2009
      finding: 'One altruistic donor started a chain of 10 kidney transplants (begun July 2007) over eight months, run non-simultaneously. N Engl J Med 360: 1096–1101.'
    - id: nota1984
      title: National Organ Transplant Act
      authors: US Congress
      year: 1984
      finding: Makes it unlawful to acquire or transfer a human organ for valuable consideration for use in transplantation.

    box ann "Ann · A" #b5482e x 320 y 520 width 290 height 150 radius 14 icon ["hospital bed","bed"] font_size 40 hidden true
    box bo "Bo · B" #3d3833 x 320 y 245 width 290 height 150 radius 14 icon ["man","person"] font_size 40 hidden true
