    box dora "Dora · A" #3d3833 x 650 y 245 width 290 height 150 radius 14 icon ["woman","person"] font_size 40 hidden true
    text nm1 "✗ no match" #b5482e x 320 y 382 font_size 30 hidden true
    text nm2 "✗ no match" #b5482e x 650 y 382 font_size 30 hidden true
    text ban "sale banned" #b5482e x 320 y 645 font_size 40 hidden true
    scratch rule work ["A takes A or O","B takes B or O"] x 855 y 382 font_size 28 hidden true
    arrow a_dora dora -> ann #87a878 style.stroke_width 4 hidden true
    arrow a_bo bo -> carl #87a878 style.stroke_width 4 hidden true
    text sameday "same day, side by side" #3d3833 x 490 y 645 font_size 36 hidden true
    box stranger "stranger" #87a878 x 82 y 520 width 140 height 150 radius 14 icon ["person","heart"] font_size 28 hidden true
    arrow a_str stranger -> ann #87a878 style.stroke_width 4 hidden true
    text next "next …" #8f887c x 920 y 245 font_size 34 hidden true
    arrow a_next #87a878 from.ref dora to.x 850 to.y 245 style.stroke_width 4 hidden true
    text chain "chain" #87a878 x 82 y 405 font_size 36 hidden true

    card title "A swap instead of a sale"

Can a market work without money? Take something nobody is allowed to buy: a kidney.
    draw ann

Ann needs one. Her husba (@draw bo@)nd Bo wants to give her one of his, but his blood type is B and hers is A. Hi (@draw nm1@)s kidney won't do.

Selling kid (@draw ban@)neys is banned in the United States, and in almost every other country.  (@highlight ann@)So Ann can't buy one either.

    erase ban

B: But in another city there's Carl. His sister Dora wants to give, and they're mismatched the other way round.
    draw carl dora nm2

A type-A patient can take a kidney from an A donor, or from an O.
    draw rule_box rule_line_1 rule_line_2

    ask "Tap the donor who could give Ann a kidney." choose ["bo","dora"] answer dora voice b pool false
        intro: Your guess first: which of the two donors could save Ann?
        right: Dora: she is type A, like Ann.
        wrong: Ann is type A, so she needs an A or O donor: here, Dora.

    erase rule

    erase nm1 nm2

(@draw a_dora@) Dora. And Bo is type B, ex (@draw a_bo@)actly what Carl needs. So the two pairs swap: each donor saves a stranger, and each patient gets a kidney.

    pause 0.4

B: Why would the operations have to happen on the same day?
    draw sameday

Because nobody can be sued into giving a kidney. If Bo's half ran first, Dora could still change her mind, so the four operations usually ran side by side.
    highlight a_bo a_dora

    erase sameday a_dora

Then came a twist. A stranger who wants to give to anyone starts a chain: her kidney to Ann,
    draw stranger a_str

Bo's to Carl,
    highlight a_bo

Dora's on to the next pair.
    draw a_next next chain

A chain needn't run all at once. In 2007 one stranger's gift set off ten transplants over eight months.
    text chain10 "1 stranger → 10 transplants" #87a878 cites ["rees2009"] x 490 y 645 font_size 48

    erase chain10

No money changes hands, and still kidneys go where they fit. Who gets what is settled by rules, not by a price.
    text rules "rules, not a price" #3d3833 x 490 y 645 font_size 44

## Running the match
sources:
    - id: gale1962
      title: College Admissions and the Stability of Marriage
      authors: Gale, D. & Shapley, L. S.
      year: 1962
      finding: 'The deferred acceptance procedure always ends in a stable matching, the best stable matching for the proposing side. American Mathematical Monthly 69(1): 9–15.'
    - id: roth1982
      title: 'The Economics of Matching: Stability and Incentives'
      authors: Roth, A. E.
      year: 1982
      finding: 'Under proposer-proposing deferred acceptance, stating true preferences is a dominant strategy for the proposers; no stable mechanism is strategy-proof for both sides. Mathematics of Operations Research 7(4): 617–628.'

    box ella "Ella\nHarbour › Hill › Bay" #2f6b8f x 190 y 555 width 330 height 120 radius 14 icon ["girl","person"] icon_side left font_size 28 hidden true
