    card title "Running the match"

B: A kidney swap is a match made without a price. Matchi (@draw ella finn gia@)ng people to school  (@draw harbour hill bay@)places is the same kind of problem, and Harbourtown has one.

B: Three pupils, three schools with one seat left each. The numbers are illustrative. Eve (@highlight ella finn gia@)ry pupil ranks the schools; every school (@highlight harbour hill bay@) ranks pupils, say by distance.
    draw seat illus parallel true

    erase seat illus

Here's the rule, from two mathematicians, David Gale and Lloyd Shapley. Round one: ev (@draw e_h f_h g_hi parallel true@)eryone applies to their first choice.
    draw r1

    ask "Which school has to turn someone away? Tap it." intro "Your turn to run it." choose ["harbour","hill","bay"] answer harbour pool false
        right: Harbour: two pupils want its one seat.
        wrong: Count the arrows into each school: two pupils want Harbour's one seat. Here, Harbour.

Harbour. It keeps Ella, whom it ranks higher, (@erase f_h@) and  (@draw x_fh@)turns Finn away. But it only holds Ella; (@draw hold@) nothing is final yet.

    erase r1 x_fh

B: Round two: Finn tries his second choice, Hill. Hill now has Gia and Finn.
    draw r2 f_hi

    ask "Hill holds one pupil. Tap the one it keeps." intro "Which one does it keep?" choose ["finn","gia"] answer finn voice b pool false
        right: Finn: Hill ranks him above Gia.
        wrong: Read Hill's own ranking: Finn comes before Gia. Here, Finn.

Finn: Hill ranks him above Gia. So Gia, w (@erase g_hi@)ho thought s (@draw x_ghi@)he was in, is bumped. That's why it' (@erase r2@)s cal (@draw deferred@)led deferred acceptance.

    erase x_ghi deferred

Round three: Gia tries Bay, and Bay takes her. Nobody is left out, so the  (@erase hold@)holds become final.
    draw r3 g_b

B: Gia wanted Hill. Isn't that unfair to her?
    highlight gia effect circle

Hill would still rather have Finn. No pupil and school would both rather be together (@erase r3@). That (@draw stable@)'s what economists call a stable match.

    erase stable

Now change one rule. Say, as Boston once did, that eve (@draw boston b_eh b_gh@)ry acceptance in round one is final.
    fade e_h f_hi g_b to 0.15

    ask "Round-one seats are final. Tap the school Finn gets." intro "Where does Finn end up?" choose ["harbour","hill","bay"] answer bay pool false
        right: Bay: Hill's seat went to Gia in round one, for good.
        wrong: After round one only Bay still has a free seat: here, Bay.

At Bay. Hill filled up with Gia in round one, though Hill ranks Finn high (@draw block block_l@)er and Finn prefers Hill: the two would both break the deal.
    draw b_fb

B: So under that rule Finn should have lied and put Hill first.
    draw lie

    erase lie boston b_eh b_gh b_fb block block_l

Under deferred acceptance he'd gain nothing by l (@draw honest@)ying. Telling the truth is safe for every pupil. That's its second gift.
    fade e_h f_hi g_b to 1

## What Roth added
sources:
    - id: roth1984
      title: 'The Evolution of the Labor Market for Medical Interns and Residents: A Case Study in Game Theory'
      authors: Roth, A. E.
      year: 1984
      finding: 'US hospitals made offers ever earlier in the 1940s (up to two years before graduation); the clearing house from 1952 used an algorithm equivalent to (hospital-proposing) deferred acceptance. Journal of Political Economy 92(6): 991–1016.'
    - id: gale1962
      title: College Admissions and the Stability of Marriage
      authors: Gale, D. & Shapley, L. S.
      year: 1962
      finding: 'Deferred acceptance always ends in a stable matching. American Mathematical Monthly 69(1): 9–15.'
    - id: roth1991
      title: 'A Natural Experiment in the Organization of Entry-Level Labor Markets: Regional Markets for New Physicians and Surgeons in the United Kingdom'
      authors: Roth, A. E.
      year: 1991
      finding: 'UK regional clearing houses that produced stable matches (Edinburgh 1969, Cardiff) stayed in use; most unstable ones (Birmingham, Edinburgh 1967, Newcastle, Sheffield) were abandoned. American Economic Review 81(3): 415–440.'
    - id: apr2005
      title: The New York City High School Match
      authors: Abdulkadiroğlu, A., Pathak, P. A. & Roth, A. E.
      year: 2005
      finding: 'About 30,000 students were assigned to a school not on their list under the old process; in the first year of the deferred-acceptance match (2003–04) about 3,000, 10 percent as many. AER Papers & Proceedings 95(2): 364–367.'
      url: https://stanford.edu/~alroth/papers/nycAEAPP.pdf
    - id: aprs2006
      title: Changing the Boston School Choice Mechanism
      authors: Abdulkadiroğlu, A., Pathak, P. A., Roth, A. E. & Sönmez, T.
      year: 2006
      finding: The Boston School Committee voted in July 2005 to replace its mechanism with student-proposing deferred acceptance. NBER Working Paper 11965.
    - id: rsu2004
      title: Kidney Exchange
      authors: Roth, A. E., Sönmez, T. & Ünver, M. U.
      year: 2004
      finding: 'Designs for exchanging kidneys between incompatible patient–donor pairs; basis of the New England Program for Kidney Exchange (2004). Quarterly Journal of Economics 119(2): 457–488.'
    - id: nobel2012
      title: The Prize in Economic Sciences 2012 – press release
      authors: Royal Swedish Academy of Sciences
      year: 2012
      finding: Awarded to Alvin E. Roth and Lloyd S. Shapley for the theory of stable allocations and the practice of market design.
      url: https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/economic-sciences/2012/press-release/

