    card title "What Roth added"

Gale and Shap (@draw event_gs@)ley proved in 1962 that this procedure always ends in a stable match. The q (@highlight event_gs@)uestion was whether real markets care.

B: American hospitals in the 1940s answere (@draw event_early@)d first. Racing each other, they hired medical students up to two years before graduation.

From 1952 a clea (@draw event_nrmp@)ring house matched them all at once, and the scramble stopped.

In 1984 Alvin Roth showed (@draw event_roth rothpic@) its rule was essentially Gale and Shapley's, found by doctors a decade before the mathematicians.

B: Lucky accident, or the reason it worked?
    highlight event_nrmp event_gs

    erase rothpic

Britain gave a test. It (@draw event_uk@)s regions set up their own clearing houses for young doctors; the sta (@highlight event_uk@)ble ones lasted, and most of the unstable ones were abandoned.

Then Roth and his colleagues began to build. (@draw event_build nycscale before@) In New York, the old school system left about thirty thousand pupils at a school they had never listed.

    ask "Click the line at your guess for the first year of the new match." on nycscale store g default "20000" pool false
        intro: Your guess: how many in the first year of the new match?
        right: About 3,000: you said {g}, close.
        wrong: You said {g}. It was about 3,000.

About three thousan (@draw tenth@)d, a tenth as many.

Boston switched its rule in 2005, and Roth's team helped launch kidney exchanges in New England.
    highlight event_build

    erase nycscale before tenth

B: So that's the addition: the economist a (@draw engineer@)s engineer, who doesn't just describe a market but designs its rules and checks they hold.

Roth sha (@draw event_nobel@)red the 2012 Nobel prize with Shapley. Gale had died four years earlier.

    erase engineer

    fade events to 0.15

    draw mathq

    ask "Show the math, or skip it?" on mathq stage own voice b
        intro: Want to see why it always works?

@math

    erase mathq

    fade events to 0.12

A blocking pair is a pupil and a school who both prefer each other to what they got. Stable means there are none.
    draw sc_box sc_line_1

B: Each rejection crosses one school off one pupil's list, for good.
    draw sc_line_2

So with n pupils and n schools, it stops after at most n times n proposals.
    draw sc_line_3

B: And no blocking pair survives: if Gia wants Hill, she asked Hill first, and Hill turned her down for someone it ranks higher.
    draw sc_line_4

    erase sc

@test

    erase mathq

    fade events to 1

## Is stable the best?
feedback:
    style: dry
    perfect: Roth would hire you to design the next match.
    good: A sound market designer in the making.
    poor: Run the proposals once more, round by round.
    none: Even Gale and Shapley started with a blank page.
sources:
    - id: acy2011
      title: 'Resolving Conflicting Preferences in School Choice: The "Boston Mechanism" Reconsidered'
      authors: Abdulkadiroğlu, A., Che, Y.-K. & Yasuda, Y.
      year: 2011
      finding: 'When schools'' priorities are coarse and ties are broken by lottery, the Boston mechanism can make every student better off in expectation than deferred acceptance, because it lets students signal how intensely they prefer schools. American Economic Review 101(1): 399–410.'
    - id: ps2008
      title: 'Leveling the Playing Field: Sincere and Sophisticated Players in the Boston Mechanism'
      authors: Pathak, P. A. & Sönmez, T.
      year: 2008
      finding: 'The Boston mechanism rewards sophisticated families at the expense of sincere ones; a fairness argument behind Boston''s 2005 switch to deferred acceptance. American Economic Review 98(4): 1636–1652.'
    - id: apr2009
      title: 'Strategy-proofness versus Efficiency in Matching with Indifferences: Redesigning the New York City High School Match'
      authors: Abdulkadiroğlu, A., Pathak, P. A. & Roth, A. E.
      year: 2009
      finding: 'In NYC data, about 4,300 more students (5.5% of 8th graders) could improve their assignment if the stability constraint were relaxed (beyond ~1,500, 1.9%, from dropping strategy-proofness). American Economic Review 99(5): 1954–1978.'
      url: https://www.nber.org/papers/w14864

