    card title "Is stable the best?"

B: Stable, and safe to be honest. Sounds like the last word. Is it?
    draw h_da rule_v da1

Not everyone thinks (@draw h_bos@) so. The Boston rule had one thing going for it: putting a (@draw bos1@) school first was a way of saying how much you care.

B: Under defe (@draw da2@)rred acceptance, a family desperate for Hill and on (@highlight h_da@)e that barely minds both just write Hill.

Some economists sh (@draw bos2@)ow that when schools rank pupils by lottery, the Boston rule can l (@highlight bos2@)eave families better off on average.

B: The reply: it rewards the families who  (@draw bos3@)know how to play it and punishes the ones who write the truth. T (@highlight h_bos@)hat's what decided Boston.

And a stable match isn't always t (@draw da3@)he best for pupils. In New York's data, about one pupil in twenty could have got a  (@highlight da3@)school they liked more, if stability were dropped.

Kidney excha (@draw kid@)nge has its limit too: it makes the most of willing donors, but there are still far more patients than kidneys.

    erase h_da h_bos rule_v da1 bos1 da2 bos2 bos3 da3 kid

How sure are we? That the pro (@draw c1 c1s@)cedure is stable and safe for honest applicants is a theorem. That s (@draw c2 c2s@)table clearing houses work better is mostly agreed. Which (@draw c3 c3s@) rule is best for schools is contested.

    quiz "A city uses deferred acceptance. Mia's daughter loves Bay but fears she won't get in. What should she list first?" intro "Three new cases."
        right: Bay. A rejection there only sends her on down her list, where every seat is still open to her.
        wrong: Being turned down by Bay can't cost her a seat further down her list: here, Bay.
        + Bay
        * A safer school
        * Leave Bay off

    hide c1 c1s c2 c2s c3 c3s

    draw sortc

    ask "Who decides who gets it: the price alone, or do both sides have to choose each other? Sort each one." on sortc store f stage own voice b
        right: Wheat, petrol and a cinema seat go to whoever pays. A college, an employer and a transplant team also have to pick you.
        wrong: {f} of {f.total} on the first try. Wheat, petrol and a cinema seat go by price; a college, a first job and a kidney need both sides to choose.

    erase sortc

    show c1 c1s c2 c2s c3 c3s

    quiz "In one city, many families put a so-so school first because its round-one seats go fast. Which rule does the city probably use?" voice b
        right: First-round places are final: that is the Boston-style rule, where an honest first choice can cost you your second.
        wrong: Only when first-round places are final can an honest favourite cost you your second choice: here, the round-one-final rule.
        + First-round places are final
        * Deferred acceptance
        * One lottery over everyone

    fade c1 c1s c2 c2s c3 c3s to 0.3

So can a market work without money? Yes. A price decid (@draw close@)es who gets what; where prices are banned, carefully designed rules can do that job. Whether some things should never be priced at all is a question of its own.

